WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:09.380
Hey Thoughty2 Here.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.950
Having an itch can be a pretty nasty thing,
severe itches can be caused by bug bites,

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.700
sunburn and poison ivy.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.971
Millions of people around the world suffer
from a skin condition called dermatitis, where

00:00:19.971 --> 00:00:25.010
an individual's skin becomes very dry and
itchy, when they come into contact with certain

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:28.490
things such as laundry detergent and jewellery.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:34.390
Some people who have atopic dermatitis, a
type of eczema, actually scratch during their

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.390
sleep.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.980
In many cases sufferers of chronic itches
will uncontrollably scratch themselves until

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:40.980
they bleed.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:46.370
According to Martin Schmelz, a neurophysiologist
at the University or Mannheim in Germany "Quality

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:51.080
of life is decreased by itch to the same degree
as it is by pain".

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.920
But people don't have nearly as much empathy
for those with a chronic itch as those with

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:56.920
pain.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.640
About 9% of people have some sort of chronic
itch problem.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.170
So finding a cure for itching would be a medical
revolution, there is actually a conference

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:10.580
dedicated to this subject called the International
Workshop for the Study of Itch, which hundreds

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.990
of scientists and researchers attend.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.050
Because even though there have many small
advances in the field, itching is still a

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.950
bit of a mystery and we're still a long way
from a cure.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:24.660
But an itch isn't just a form of mild pain,
pain causes us to recoil.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.920
Whereas an itch just gives us an unpleasant
desire to scratch.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.349
Itching has it's own matrix of nerves and
chemicals, completely separate from those

00:01:33.349 --> 00:01:34.800
used to relay pain.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.619
But why do we itch?

00:01:36.619 --> 00:01:42.570
Luckily for us itching can only occur in the
top two layers of our skin, we can't get internal

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.239
itches, thank god.

00:01:44.239 --> 00:01:48.310
Animals develop itches when they have bugs
and parasites on their skin, itching is a

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:53.420
natural defence mechanism against these insects,
which can give the animals infections.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.330
Animals scratch to remove the bugs because
their body has signalled to them, via an itch,

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.840
that something very small is attacking them.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.600
One widely adopted theory is that we itch
because we still have a primate instinct to

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.600
do so.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.740
A defence mechanism such as this is an evolutionary
advantage, so it make sense for our bodies

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:13.740
to keep it.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.440
After all we aren't safe from parasites either.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:22.070
So ultimately we itch to defend ourselves
from parasites and infection or anything which

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.140
could irritate our skin, such as stinging
nettles.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:30.310
When we get an itch, dedicated cells around
the affected area release an organic nitrogen

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.330
compound called histamine.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:37.310
These histamines send signals to our brain,
more specifically the cerebral cortex which

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.230
controls our conscious thoughts, our cerebral
cortex then tells us we have an itch.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.530
Antihistamines work by stopping the histamines
from reaching their receptors.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.730
But why does scratching relieve an itch?

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:54.740
Scratching the skin blocks activity of the
cells in the spinothalamic tract, a sensory

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.400
pathway originating in the spinal cord.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.990
This lack of activity prevents the spinal
cord from sending signals to our brain that

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.020
we have an itch.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.240
Therefore the itch stops.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:09.960
People who have had a limb amputated experience
a syndrome called "phantom limb", where the

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.150
amputee feels severe itching where the limb
used to be.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.120
This phenomenon occurs in over 90% of amputees.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:23.069
Itching is contagious, when we see someone
else scratching themselves we develop itches

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.069
from nowhere.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:29.230
In a recent studies, by researchers at the
universities of Hull and Sussex, in the UK,

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.450
participants where shown videos of people
scratching their bodies.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:37.510
64% of the test subjects scratched themselves
whilst watching the videos.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.880
You can even manifest itches by just thinking
about itches, in fact you're probably feeling

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.860
itchy whilst watching this video.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:45.860
But why?

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:51.060
In MRI scans researchers found that when people
see others scratch, the parts of their brains

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.540
associated with having an itch were activated.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.140
It's not empathy that causes this, we don't
feel sorry for the person with an itch.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.730
It's neuroticism; neuroticism is the tendency
to feel negative emotions.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:08.410
In the studies the people who were the most
neurotic suffered the most from contagious

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.320
itching.

